      <h1>Impedance Testing</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Testing Methods · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Impedance testing measures a battery's <strong>frequency-dependent
        resistance</strong> — the opposition to an AC signal at one or several frequencies. Unlike the single-number
        conductance read, an impedance measurement (especially swept over frequencies, as in EIS) separates the
        battery's <strong>ohmic, charge-transfer and diffusion</strong> components, giving a more diagnostic picture
        of where the battery is degrading.
      </div>

      <h2>Impedance vs resistance vs conductance</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>What it captures</th><th>Detail level</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Conductance</strong></td><td>Single AC read, inverse of resistance</td><td>One number — health proxy</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>DC resistance</strong></td><td>Resistance under load or pulse</td><td>One number</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Impedance (EIS)</strong></td><td>Resistance across frequencies</td><td>Separates internal processes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Conductance is the field-tool read; impedance spectroscopy is the laboratory read. The field tool tells you
        <em>that</em> the battery is degrading; the impedance sweep begins to tell you <em>where</em> — see
        <a href="/resistance-conductance/">resistance and conductance</a> for the single-number version.
      </p>

      <h2>What the frequency sweep reveals</h2>
      <p>
        At high frequencies the signal sees mostly the <strong>ohmic path</strong> (connectors, electrolyte
        resistance); at mid frequencies it sees the <strong>charge-transfer</strong> at the electrode interface; at
        low frequencies it sees <strong>diffusion</strong> inside the active material. Each band reacts differently
        to sulfation, corrosion and drying, so the swept measurement separates the aging mechanisms instead of
        blending them into one number.
      </p>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>My position:</strong> Impedance testing is the difference between knowing a battery is failing and
        knowing why it is failing — the field tools triage, the frequency sweep diagnoses, and the two belong
        together in any serious battery evaluation.</p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> A single resistance number is a health score without a cause. The impedance sweep
        decomposes that score into its mechanisms, which is exactly what warranty, research and large-fleet decisions
        need — the diagnosis, not just the verdict.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is my analysis, not a verified fact or purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
